\section{Windows Personality}
WINE can basically be run as a server. This is probably to act as a
drop-in replacement for the Western Linux desktop.

\section{OpenHarmony Personality}
While economic, OpenHarmony also has notable technology. The potential
for gain may be limited barring the ability to interoperate at a peer
level. It's unclear whether the limitations would be mechanistic or
policy-driven, where only the former would be tractable.

\section{Zircon Personality}
This might be an economic reach toward the Google ecosystem, though
there are questions about how broadly Fuchsia, whose kernel Zircon is,
is being deployed. There are similar questions about exclusion from
interoperation via policy as OpenHarmony.
